  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Using the sum and product rules of differentiation, on an exponent and a log function for higher order</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Differentiate the following function.</p>
<p>\(\quad \displaystyle f(x) = {@fx@}\)</p>
<ol class="HELM_parts">
<li>
<p>\( f'(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>\( f''(x)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>\( f'''(x)=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>\( f'''({@x_val@})=\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>
</li>
<ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To differentiate the function \(f(x) = {@fx@}\), we'll need to apply the rules of differentiation. </p>

<p>Using the sum and product rules of differentiation, we can differentiate each term separately:</p>
<ol class="HELM_parts">
<li>
<p>First Derivative \(f'(x)\):</p>
<p><b>(i)</b> Differentiate \(\displaystyle {@fx1@}\):</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \left({@fx1@}\right) = {@diff_fx1@}\)</p>

<p><b>(ii)</b> Differentiate \( \displaystyle {@fx2*fx3@} \) using the product rule:</p>
<p>Let \(\displaystyle u = {@fx2@}\) and \(v ={@fx3@}\). Then, we have:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle u' = {@diff_fx2@}\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle v' ={@diff_fx3@}\)</p>
<p>Now, applying the product rule, we get:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  \frac{d}{dx} \left({@fx2*fx3@}\right) = u'v + uv' = ({@diff_fx2@})({@fx3@}) + ({@fx2@})({@diff_fx3@}) = {@diff_fx4@}\)</p>

<p>So, the first derivative \(\displaystyle f'(x)={@fx@}\) is:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle f'(x) = {@ta1@}\)</p>
<p>For the second and third derivatives we will apply the same steps above:</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Second Derivative \(f''(x)\):</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ta1@}\right) = {@ta2@} \)</p>
<p>So, \(\displaystyle  f''(x) ={@ta2@} \)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Third Derivative \(f'''(x)\):</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ta2@}\right) = {@ta3@} \)</p>
<p>So, \( \displaystyle f'''(x) ={@ta3@} \)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Evaluate \(\displaystyle f'''({@x_val@})\):</p>
<p>To find \(\displaystyle f'''({@x_val@})\), substitute \(\displaystyle x = {@x_val@}\) into the third derivative:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  f'''({@x_val@}) = {@ta4@} \)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  \qquad \quad \approx {@decimalplaces(ta4,2)@} \)</p>
</li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
